+From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: prospero-win <prospero-win@invalid>
+Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 09:00:00 +0000
+Subject: [PATCH] kernelbase: PS5: do the work of fxc.exe, the shader
+ compiler a game starts, in its own process.
+
+A PS5 title is a single process, so CreateProcess fails there with
+STATUS_NOT_SUPPORTED. Far Cry builds most of its shaders at start by
+running the shader compiler it ships, about a hundred times:
+
+  fxc.exe /T vs_1_1 /Zpr /DCGC=0 /Fc $$out.cg $$in.cg
+
+and reads the assembly listing back. Every one of those starts failed
+("CreateProcess failed", "Couldn't create CG pixel program"), and the
+game drew without its fog, lighting, shadow and detail shaders: blurry,
+repeating textures.
+
+When the start of fxc.exe fails with STATUS_NOT_SUPPORTED, its work is
+done in the calling process instead, with Wine's d3dcompiler_47: the
+options fxc takes (/T, /E, /D, /Zpr, /Zpc, /Od, /Vd, /Gpp, /Zi), the
+file named, the listing (/Fc) and the code (/Fo), relative to the
+folder the tool would have started in. The listing is the
+disassembly, after the parameters and registers of the code's constant
+table, as fxc writes them. The caller gets a process that has already
+ended: a signalled event for the process and one for its thread, whose
+exit code (0 when it compiled, 1 otherwise) GetExitCodeProcess returns.
+The first one is reported once. Where a process can be created nothing
+changes.
